{"version":"6.9","hitCount":1,"request":{"queryString":"EXT_ID:42802368 AND SRC:MED","internalQuery":"EXT_ID:42802368 AND SRC:MED","resultType":"CORE","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":25,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"42802368","source":"MED","pmid":"42802368","pmcid":"PMC13617179","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13617179"]},"doi":"10.1111/cch.70346","title":"Who Uses Services Overall and Within High-Need Families? Social Inequalities in Access to Early Support Services in Southern Germany.","authorString":"Herrmann S, Nehring I, Mall V, Friedmann A.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Herrmann S","lastName":"Herrmann","firstName":"Sonja","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9149-3765"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Chair of Social Pediatrics, TUM School of Medicine and Health, Technical University of Munich, Munich, Germany."},{"affiliation":"Faculty of Psychology and Educational Sciences, Ludwig Maximilian University, Munich, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Nehring I","lastName":"Nehring","firstName":"Ina","initials":"I","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8190-687X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Chair of Social Pediatrics, TUM School of Medicine and Health, Technical University of Munich, Munich, Germany."},{"affiliation":"HMU Health and Medical University, Potsdam, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Mall V","lastName":"Mall","firstName":"Volker","initials":"V","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Chair of Social Pediatrics, TUM School of Medicine and Health, Technical University of Munich, Munich, Germany."},{"affiliation":"German Center for Child and Adolescent Health (DZKJ), Munich Partner Site, Munich, Germany."},{"affiliation":"kbo Children's Centre, Munich, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Friedmann A","lastName":"Friedmann","firstName":"Anna","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5359-4760"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Chair of Social Pediatrics, TUM School of Medicine and Health, Technical University of Munich, Munich, Germany."},{"affiliation":"German Center for Child and Adolescent Health (DZKJ), Munich Partner Site, Munich, Germany."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5359-4760"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8190-687X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9149-3765"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"5","volume":"52","journalIssueId":4238805,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Child Care Health Dev","title":"Child: care, health and development","essn":"1365-2214","issn":"0305-1862","isoabbreviation":"Child Care Health Dev","nlmid":"7602632"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-09-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e70346","abstractText":"<h4>Objectives</h4>Reducing inequalities in early childhood support is a central public health and prevention challenge because services are most effective when families with high psychosocial burden can access them. This study examined a potential need-access gap in Early Support Services (Frühe Hilfen) in Southern Germany by analysing overall service use and use within a subgroup of highly burdened parents.<h4>Materials and methods</h4>Cross-sectional data from the Junge Familien in Bayern (JuFaBY) cohort of families with young children were used. High need was defined as membership in the highest cut-off category of the Eltern-Belastungs-Inventar (EBI), indicating clinically significant parenting stress. Multivariable logistic regression models with robust standard errors were estimated, adjusting for socio-economic position, family structure, parental mental health, parenting behaviour difficulties, child health, childcare hours, labour status, survey year and non-linear child age effects.<h4>Results</h4>In the full sample (N = 20 216), Early Support use was positively associated with several need indicators, including parenting stress, parenting behaviour difficulties, single parenthood and chronic child illness. A socio-economic gradient was also observed: Lower household income was associated with higher uptake, while higher parental education was associated with higher use. In the high-need subgroup (N = 3232), income differences attenuated, but disparities by parental education and migration proxy persisted.<h4>Conclusion</h4>Early Support Services appear broadly need-responsive, while selective access barriers may remain among families with high support needs. In particular, educational and migration-related differences in uptake within highly burdened families indicate potential inequalities in access despite comparable levels of need.","affiliation":"Chair of Social Pediatrics, TUM School of Medicine and Health, Technical University of Munich, Munich, Germany.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cross-Sectional Studies"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Parenting","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PX","qualifierName":"psychology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Parents","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PX","qualifierName":"psychology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Social Support"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Socioeconomic Factors"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Child, Preschool"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Infant"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Child Health Services","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"SN","qualifierName":"statistics & numerical data"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Health Services Needs and Demand"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Health Services Accessibility","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"SN","qualifierName":"statistics & numerical data"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Germany","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"EP","qualifierName":"epidemiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Female"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Male"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Healthcare Disparities"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Low Socioeconomic Status"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Socioeconomic Disparities in Health"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Germany","Social Inequalities","Parenting Stress","Service Utilization","Psychosocial Burden","Early Support Services"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/cch.70346"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13617179"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13617179?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-09-28","dateOfCreation":"2026-09-28","dateOfRevision":"2026-09-30","firstPublicationDate":"2026-09-01","firstIndexDate":"2026-09-28","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-09-29","hasEvaluations":"N"}]}}